+## Status and evidence
+- 2026-10-31: **Stopped before fixing. The bug does not reproduce, so `fix/ratio-rounding` was not created and nothing was committed there.**
+- Findings (against `main` 6bda240, which matches `orchestration` 84a7b3d except for this doc):
+  - The saved screen's headline ratio is `1:{{ "%.2f" | format(ratio(shot)) }}` (`app/templates/saved.html:7`). For 18 in / 37 out (37/18 = 2.0556) it renders **`1:2.06`**, not `1:2.0`. It has used two decimals since the template was added (59f17b0).
+  - Every other place the shot ratio is shown agrees on two decimals and gives `1:2.06`: `today.html:20`, `shot.html:8`, the `_macros.html:5` shot row, the live ratio on Log shot (`app.js:311`, `toFixed(2)`), and the offline saved view (`app.js:479`, `toFixed(2)`). The only one-decimal ratio text is the dial-in advice in `src/coffeedata/shots.py:436` (`:.1f`), which gives `1:2.1`. That text is the user's expected value, and it is correct.
+  - The only `1:2.0` on the saved screen is the **y-axis gridline label of the target chart** (`app/views.py:116-119`, ticks 1:1.8/2.0/2.2/2.4). The shot's mark (2.056) sits just above the 1:2.0 gridline. The likeliest explanation is that this label was read as the shot's ratio. A second possibility can't be checked here because `$COFFEEDATA_LAKE` is unset and I have no access to the user's shot: the stored dose was not 18.0 (for example 18.5 gives 37/18.5 = 1:2.00). The saved screen also shows dose and yield as "18.0 → 37.0 g", which would show which one it was.
+  - There is no rounding defect to fix. Changing the display to one decimal would be a design change for every ratio in the app, and it would break the existing assertion `"1:2.02" in saved` (`tests/test_app.py:129`). That falls under the Scope's "stop and report rather than expanding scope".
